    "text": "{\"anomaly_detected\": true, \"confidence\": 0.6, \"anomalies\": [{\"category\": \"bridged_nets\", \"components\": [\"U2\"], \"pins\": [\"U2.1\"], \"nets\": [\"+3.3V\"], \"observed\": \"PCA9685 address-select pin A0 (U2 pin 1) is on net +3.3V, while its other address pins A1 to A5 (pins 2, 3, 4, 5, 24) are on GND. The address bit A0 is therefore tied high.\", \"expected\": \"With A0 tied to GND, as the other address pins are, the PCA9685 responds at 0x40 as the specification states. Tying A0 high gives 0x41.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A0\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A1\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"PWM/servo driver | FCCPeripherals | NXP PCA9685PW (I2C 16-ch PWM), I2C addr 0x40\"}], \"impact\": \"The PCA9685 will not answer at its specified address 0x40, so the servo/PWM outputs cannot be driven by firmware that expects 0x40.\", \"confidence\": 0.5}, {\"category\": \"open_connection\", \"components\": [\"FCC1\", \"NAVC1\"], \"pins\": [\"FCC1.11\", \"FCC1.27\", \"FCC1.50\", \"FCC1.75\", \"FCC1.100\", \"NAVC1.19\", \"NAVC1.32\", \"NAVC1.48\", \"NAVC1.64\"], \"nets\": [\"unconnected-(FCC1-VDD-Pad11)\", \"unconnected-(NAVC1-VDD-Pad32)\"], \"observed\": \"All VDD supply pins of both MCUs are on single-pin nets. None of them is connected to +3V3, +3.3V or any other rail. The +3V3 net that feeds the MCU decoupling and I2C pull-ups has no regulator output on it.\", \"expected\": \"The specification says 3V3 feeds both MCUs. Every VDD pin of an STM32 must be tied to the 3.3 V rail.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"216\\\") (name \\\"unconnected-(FCC1-VDD-Pad11)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\")\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"276\\\") (name \\\"unconnected-(NAVC1-VDD-Pad19)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\")\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"3V3 feeds both MCUs (FCC, NAVC) and all I2C sensor ICs\"}], \"impact\": \"Both MCUs would be unpowered or unreliably powered, so the board would not boot.\", \"confidence\": 0.3}]), \"summary\": \"The netlist shows the PCA9685 address pin A0 tied to +3.3V, which changes its I2C address from the specified 0x40 to 0x41. All VDD pins of both MCUs are also unconnected, which conflicts with the specification that 3V3 feeds the MCUs. The first finding is the more clearly supported of the two.\"}",
